 }-2,\\\\\nL_3 &: \\text{through }(n-1,2)\\text{ and }(n-2,1) &&\\text{slope }1,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nhave slopes \\(-\\tfrac12,\\,-2,\\;1\\), none of which is forbidden.\nTogether they contain every point of the three columns.\nAdding the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines\nwith exactly three sunny ones covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\nFor \\(n=3\\) the vertical lines are absent and the three sunny lines alone\nsatisfy the requirements.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Final answer\n\nFrom (8) we have \\(k\\le3\\); the case \\(k=2\\) is excluded by the argument of\nSection 4.  The explicit constructions of Section 5 show that the remaining\nvalues \\(k=0,1,3\\) are attainable for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge3\\text{ the admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0,\\;1,\\;3.}\n\\]"
