e \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).\n   The remaining three columns are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   C_{n-2}&:\\;(n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3),\\\\\n   C_{n-1}&:\\;(n-1,1),(n-1,2),\\\\\n   C_{n}&:\\;(n,1).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: \\text{through }(n,1)\\text{ and }(n-2,2) &&\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12,\\\\\n   L_2 &: \\text{through }(n-1,1)\\text{ and }(n-2,3) &&\\text{slope }-2,\\\\\n   L_3 &: \\text{through }(n-1,2)\\text{ and }(n-2,1) &&\\text{slope }1,\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   are all sunny (their slopes are \\(-\\tfrac12, -2, 1\\), none of which is\n   \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  Together they contain every point of the three columns.\n   Adding the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines gives a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines\n   with exactly three sunny ones covering all required points.\n   For \\(n=3\\) the vertical lines are absent and the three sunny lines above\n   already form the required configuration.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 forces \\(k\\le3\\); the separate argument excludes \\(k=2\\); the\n   explicit constructions show that \\(k=0,1,3\\) are attainable for every\n   integer \\(n\\ge3\\).  Hence the complete set of admissible values is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{0,\\;1,\\;3 } .\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal),\n\\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Reduction to a triangular sub‑set\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and\ncontaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nReplace every non‑sunny line by a vertical line (if it is horizontal or of\nslope \\(-1\\) we can rotate the whole picture by \\(90^{\\circ}\\) and obtain a\nvertical line cov