cannot contain three points of \\(T_k\\); it meets\n\\(T_k\\) in at most two points. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Upper bound for \\(k\\)\n\nBy Lemma 1 the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(T_k\\).\nUsing (3),\n\n\\[\n2k\\ge\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k+1\\le4\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk\\le3 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nHence **\\(k\\ge4\\) is impossible**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. The case \\(k=2\\) is also impossible  \n\n\\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).\nAny two of these points share a row, a column or an anti‑diagonal,\nso a sunny line (which cannot be horizontal, vertical or have slope \\(-1\\))\ncan contain at most one of them.\nConsequently at least three sunny lines would be needed, contradicting the\nrequirement of exactly two.  Thus **\\(k=2\\) cannot occur**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Constructions for the remaining values\n\n*\\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \nAll are non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  \nUse the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\; y-1 = -\\frac{n-2}{\\,n-1\\,}(x-n),\n\\]\n\nwhich passes through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,n-1)\\).  \nIts slope \\(-\\dfrac{n-2}{n-1}\\) differs from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus exactly one\nsunny line is used and all points are covered.\n\n*\\(k=3\\).*  \nEmploy the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).\nThe three remaining columns are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nC_{n-2}&:\\;(n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3),\\\\\nC_{n-1}&:\\;(n-1,1),(n-1,2),\\\\\nC_{n}&:\\;(n,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe three sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: \\text{through }(n,1)\\text{ and }(n-2,2) &&\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12,\\\\\nL_2 &: \\text{through }(n-1,1)\\text{ and }(n-2,3) &&\\text{slope }-2,\\\\\nL_3 &: