\(S_n\\).  \n   The points that are still uncovered form a set isomorphic to \\(S_k\\):\n   after deleting the first \\(v=n-k\\) columns we are left with the columns\n   \\(n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\); after the translation\n   \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-(n-k),b)\\) this set becomes exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   S_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the remaining \\(k\\) lines (which are precisely the sunny ones) must cover **all** points of \\(S_k\\).\n\n4. **What can \\(k\\) be?**  \n   Apply Lemma 1 to the triangle \\(S_k\\).  \n   Inside \\(S_k\\) a sunny line contains at most  \n\n   \\[\n   m_k:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   points.  Consequently\n\n   \\[\n   k\\;m_k\\;\\ge\\;|S_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Inequality (5) is equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\bigl(2m_k-(k+1)\\bigr)\\ge 0 .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(m_k\\le\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) with equality **only when \\(k\\) is odd**, (5) forces  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\ \\text{to be odd}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover, when \\(k\\) is odd we have \\(m_k=\\dfrac{k+1}{2}\\) and (5) becomes an equality; therefore every sunny line must contain exactly \\(m_k\\) points of \\(S_k\\).\n\n5. **Excluding \\(k\\ge5\\).**  \n   Assume that \\(k\\ge5\\) is odd.  \n   By Lemma 2 each sunny line meets the first row of \\(S_k\\) in at most one point; the first row contains exactly \\(k\\) points, so each of the \\(k\\) sunny lines meets the first row **in exactly one point**.  \n   The same argument applied to the first column shows that each sunny line also meets the first column in exactly one point.  \n   Consequently every sunny line contains **exactly two** points of \\(S_k\\) (one from the first row and one from the first column) together with the remaining \\(m_k-2=\\dfrac{k-3}{2}\\) points that lie strictly inside the triangle.\n\n   Consider the set of interior points\n   \\[\n   I_k=\\{(a,b)\\in S_k\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\},\n   \\qquad |I_k|=\\frac{(k-2)(k-1)}2 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   The \\(k\\) sunny lines together can cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl(\\frac{k-3}{2}\\Bigr)=\\frac{k(k-3)}2\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   interior points.  Because of (7),\n\n   \\[\n   |I_k|-\\frac{k(k-3)}2\n   =\\frac{(k-2)(k-1)}2-\\frac{k(k-3)}2\n   =\\frac{k-2}{2}>0\\qquad(k\\ge5).\n   \\]\n\n   Hence there is at least one interior point of \\(S_k\\) that cannot be covered by any sunny line – a contradiction.  \n   Therefore **no odd \\(k\\ge5\\) is possible**.\n\n6. **The only admissible odd values are \\(k=1\\) and \\(k=3\\).**  \n   For \\(k=1\\) the triangle \\(S_1\\) consists of the single point \\((1,1)\\); a single sunny line through that point (for instance \\(y=\\tfrac12x\\)) suffices.  \n   For \\(k=3\\) an explicit covering of \\(S_3\\) by three sunny lines is:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32\\quad\\text{through }(3,1),(1,2),\\\\\n   L_2 &: y=-2x+5\\quad\\text{through }(2,1),(1,3),\\\\\n   L_3 &: y=x\\quad\\text{through }(1,1),(2,2).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Each line is sunny (slopes \\(-\\tfrac12,-2,1\\)) and together they contain the six points of \\(S_3\\).\n\n7. **Putting everything together.**  \n   Starting from the construction in step 3 we obtain a configuration for any admissible \\(k\\):\n\n   * choose the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\);\n   * on the remaining triangle \\(S_k\\) use the covering described in step 6 (which exists exactly for \\(k=0,1,3\\)).\n\n   Thus for every \\(n\\ge3\\) we have configurations with exactly \\(k=0,1,3\\) sunny lines, and the impossibility arguments above show that no other value of \\(k\\) can occur.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\,}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its direction is different from the three forbidden directions\n\\((1,0),\\;(0,1),\\;(1,-1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (capacity of a sunny line)\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line.  Write its slope as a reduced fraction \\(\\dfrac{p}{q}\\) with\n\\(q>0\\) and \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\).  All integer points of \\(L\\) are\n\n\\[\n(x_0+mq,\\;y_0+mp),\\qquad m\\in\\mathbb Z,\n\\]\n\nfor some integer point \\((x_0,y_0)\\in L\\).  When we pass from one lattice point of\n\\(L\\) to the next, the sum \\(a+b\\) changes by \\(|p|+|q|\\ge2\\) (because the line is\nneither horizontal, vertical nor of slope \\(-1\\)).  Inside the triangle\n\\(S_n\\) the quantity \\(a+b\\) ranges from \\(2\\) to \\(n+1\\); therefore at most\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) lattice points of \\(S_n\\) can lie on \\(L\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{A sunny line contains at most }\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\text{ points of }S_n.}\n\\tag{L1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (first‑row/first‑column restriction)\n\nThe first row of \\(S_n\\) is \\(R_1=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\\) and the first column is\n\\(C_1=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\).  \nBecause a sunny line is not horizontal, it meets \\(R_1\\) in at most one point; because it is not vertical, it meets \\(C_1\\) in at most one point.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{A sunny line meets }R_1\\text{ and }C_1\\text{ in at most one point each.}}\n\\tag{L2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Reduction to a smaller triangle\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and that exactly\n\\(k\\) of them are sunny.  Let \\(v\\) be the number of vertical (non‑sunny) lines.\nReplacing any horizontal or diagonal non‑sunny line by a vertical one can only\nincrease the number of *new* points covered, so we may suppose that all\nnon‑sunny lines are vertical.  The vertical lines can be taken to be\n\\(x=1,\\dots ,x=v\\); they cover\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{v}(n+1-i)=v(n+1)-\\frac{v(v+1)}2\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(S_n\\).  The points that remain uncovered are precisely the points\nwith \\(x\\ge v+1\\); after the translation \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-v,b)\\) they form the set\n\n\\[\nS_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\qquad k=n-v .\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\nThus the remaining \\(k\\) lines (which are exactly the sunny ones) must cover **all**\npoints of \\(S_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### A necessary condition on \\(k\\)\n\nApply Lemma 1 to the triangle \\(S_k\\).  Inside \\(S_k\\) a sunny line contains at most\n\n\\[\nm_k:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\npoints.  Consequently\n\n\\[\nk\\,m_k\\;\\ge\\;|S_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k\\) is even, then \\(m_k=\\dfrac{k}{2}\\) and the left‑hand side of (5.2) equals\n\\(\\dfrac{k^2}{2}<\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2\\); contradiction.  Hence **\\(k\\) must be odd**.\n\nWhen \\(k\\) is odd we have \\(m_k=\\dfrac{k+1}{2}\\) and (5.2) becomes an equality,\nso every sunny line must contain exactly \\(\\dfrac{k+1}{2}\\) points of \\(S_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Excluding odd \\(k\\ge5\\)\n\nAssume now that \\(k\\ge5\\) is odd.  \nBy Lemma 2 each sunny line meets the first row of \\(S_k\\) in at most one point,\nand the first row contains exactly \\(k\\) points; because we have exactly \\(k\\)\nsunny lines, each line meets the first row **in exactly one point**.  The same\nargument applied to the first column shows that each sunny line also meets the\nfirst column in exactly one point.\n\nHence every sunny line already contains two points of \\(S_k\\) (one from the first\nrow, one from the first column).  Since a sunny line must contain\n\\(\\dfrac{k+1}{2}=2+\\dfrac{k-3}{2}\\) points altogether, it can contribute at most\n\\(\\dfrac{k-3}{2}\\) *interior* points (points with both coordinates at least 2).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nI_k=\\{(a,b)\\in S_k\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\},\n\\qquad |I_k|=\\frac{(k-2)(k-1)}2 .\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n\nThe \\(k\\) sunny lines together can cover at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot\\frac{k-3}{2}= \\frac{k(k-3)}2\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]\n\ninterior points.  Comparing (6.1) and (6.2),\n\n\\[\n|I_k|-\\frac{k(k-3)}2\n= \\frac{(k-2)(k-1)}2-\\frac{k(k-3)}2\n= \\frac{k-2}{2}>0\\qquad(k\\ge5).\n\\]\n\nThus there is at least one interior point of \\(S_k\\) that cannot be covered by any\nsunny line – a contradiction.  Therefore **no odd \\(k\\ge5\\) can occur**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### The only possible values of \\(k\\)\n\nFrom the previous paragraph the only admissible odd values are \\(k=1\\) and\n\\(k=3\\).  Together with the trivial case \\(k=0\\) (all lines vertical) we obtain\n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Explicit constructions\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,n\\).  All are non‑sunny and they\n  cover every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \n  The remaining column \\(x=n\\) consists of the single point \\((n,1)\\); the line\n  \\(y=\\tfrac12x\\) (any sunny line passing through \\((n,1)\\)) covers it.\n  Thus exactly one sunny line is used.\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  \n  The uncovered part is the triangle \\(S_3\\); it is covered by the three sunny\n  lines\n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_1 &: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32\\quad\\text{through }(3,1),(1,2),\\\\\n  L_2 &: y=-2x+5\\quad\\text{through }(2,1),(1,3),\\\\\n  L_3 &: y=x\\quad\\text{through }(1,1),(2,2).\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  Their slopes \\(-\\tfrac12,-2,1\\) are none of the forbidden ones, so all three\n  are sunny.  Together with the vertical lines we obtain a family of \\(n\\) distinct\n  lines containing exactly three sunny ones and covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince we have exhibited constructions for \\(k=0,1,3\\) and have proved that no\nother non‑negative integer \\(k\\) can satisfy the requirements, the answer is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\,}.\n\\]"
