            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\,}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Reduction to a triangular set.**  \n   Write  \n   \\[\n   T_{m}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_{m}|=\\frac{m(m+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   If we use \\(v\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=v\\) (all non‑sunny) then the points that are still uncovered form a set congruent to \\(T_{k}\\) with \\(k=n-v\\).  \n   Consequently the original problem is equivalent to the following one:\n\n   > For a given integer \\(k\\ge 0\\) decide whether the points of \\(T_{k}\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) **sunny** lines.\n\n2. **A bound for a sunny line.**  \n   Let a sunny line have reduced slope \\(\\dfrac{p}{q}\\;(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1)\\).  \n   The integer points on the line are\n   \\[\n   (x_{0}+mq,\\;y_{0}+mp),\\qquad m\\in\\mathbb Z .\n   \\]\n   Because the line is not horizontal, vertical nor of slope \\(-1\\), we have\n   \\[\n   p\\neq0,\\;q\\neq0,\\;|p|\\neq|q|.\n   \\]\n   Hence when we move from one lattice point of the line to the next,\n   \\[\n   (a+b)\\;\\text{changes by}\\;|p|+|q|\\ge 2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   Inside the triangle \\(T_{k}\\) the quantity \\(a+b\\) ranges from \\(2\\) to \\(k+1\\); therefore a sunny line can contain at most  \n\n   \\[\n   M(k):=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   points of \\(T_{k}\\).\n\n3. **Parity restriction.**  \n   If \\(k\\) is even, then \\(M(k)=k/2\\) and from (2) we obtain\n   \\[\n   k\\;M(k)=\\frac{k^{2}}2<\\frac{k(k+1)}2=|T_{k}|,\n   \\]\n   which contradicts the requirement that the \\(k\\) lines cover all \\(|T_{k}|\\) points.  \n   Hence **\\(k\\) must be odd** (or \\(k=0\\)).  \n\n4. **What happens for odd \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k=2t+1\\;(t\\ge0)\\).  Then \\(M(k)=t+1\\) and the inequality in (2) becomes an equality:\n   \\[\n   k\\,M(k)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2=|T_{k}|.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Consequently every covering of \\(T_{k}\\) by \\(k\\) sunny lines must be *perfect*: each line contains exactly \\(t+1\\) points of \\(T_{k}\\).\n\n   Denote by \\(R=\\{(i,1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}\\) the first row and by\n   \\(C=\\{(1,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k\\}\\) the first column.  \n   By Lemma 2 a sunny line meets \\(R\\) in at most one point and meets \\(C\\) in at most one point.  \n   Because \\(|R|=|C|=k\\) and we have exactly \\(k\\) lines, **every line meets \\(R\\) in exactly one point and meets \\(C\\) in exactly one point**, the point \\((1,1)\\) being the unique point that belongs to both sets.\n\n   Hence each line contains\n   * one point of \\(R\\),\n   * one point of \\(C\\) (different from the point of \\(R\\) unless the line is the one that passes through \\((1,1)\\)),\n   * and the remaining \\(t-1\\) points are interior points, i.e. points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2\\).\n\n   The number of interior points of \\(T_{k}\\) is\n   \\[\n   |T_{k}|-(2k-1)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2-2k+1=\\frac{(k-1)(k-2)}2 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   The total number of interior points that can be supplied by the \\(k\\) lines is\n   \\[\n   (k-1)\\bigl(t-1\\bigr)+\\bigl(t\\bigr)\n   =\\frac{(k-1)(k-2)}2 ,\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   exactly the amount required by (4).  Thus the counting constraints are *tight*; a covering can exist only if the interior points can be partitioned into the prescribed groups of size \\(t-1\\) (for the \\(k-1\\) lines that do not contain \\((1,1)\\)) and size \\(t\\) (for the line that contains \\((1,1)\\)).\n\n5. **Impossibility for \\(k\\ge5\\).**  \n   Suppose \\(k\\ge5\\) (so \\(t\\ge2\\)).  Consider the interior point \\((2,2)\\).  \n   Any sunny line that contains \\((2,2)\\) and a point of the first row must have the form\n   \\[\n   y-2 = \\frac{p}{q}\\,(x-2),\\qquad (i,1)\\in L,\n   \\]\n   which forces \\(\\frac{p}{q}= \\frac{1-2}{i-2}= \\frac{-1}{i-2}\\).  \n   Hence the slope is \\(-\\dfrac1{i-2}\\).  For \\(i\\ge3\\) this slope is different from \\(-1,0,\\infty\\) and therefore admissible; for \\(i=2\\) the line would be horizontal, which is forbidden.  Consequently the only possible row‑point that can be paired with \\((2,2)\\) is \\((i,1)\\) with \\(i\\ge3\\).\n\n   An analogous argument with the interior point \\((3,2)\\) shows that the only possible row‑point that can be paired with \\((3,2)\\) is \\((i,1)\\) with \\(i\\ge4\\); and with \\((2,3)\\) we obtain \\(i\\ge4\\) as well.  Thus the three interior points \\((2,2),(2,3),(3,2)\\) would have to be assigned to three *different* row‑points, namely \\((3,1),(4,1),(5,1)\\) (or larger).  But there are only \\(k-2\\) row‑points whose index is at least \\(3\\); when \\(k=5\\) this number equals \\(3\\), and the assignment forces the line that contains \\((1,1)\\) to miss one of the three interior points, contradicting the required interior distribution (5).  For any larger odd \\(k\\) the shortage becomes even more severe.  Hence **no covering exists for any odd \\(k\\ge5\\).**\n\n6. **Existence for the remaining values.**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); all are non‑sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \n   The only uncove