            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** iff it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the\nline \\(x+y=0\\); i.e. it is of one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\nx=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c\\qquad(c\\in\\mathbb R).\n\\tag{A}\n\\]\n\nA **sunny** line is any line that is not of the forms (A).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Geometry of a sunny line  \n\nWrite a sunny line \\(L\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with slope \\(m\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\nLet \\(m=\\dfrac{p}{q}\\) be a reduced fraction (\\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;\n\\gcd(p,q)=1\\)).  Because \\(m\\neq0\\) we have \\(p\\neq0\\); because\n\\(m\\neq-1\\) we have \\(p\\neq -q\\).\n\nAll integer points of \\(L\\) are obtained from a single integer point\n\\((x_0,y_0)\\) by\n\n\\[\n(x_0+mq,\\;y_0+mp),\\qquad m\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nHence when we move from one lattice point of \\(L\\) to the next, the sum\n\\(a+b\\) changes by  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta (a+b)=p+q .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nInside the triangle \\(S_n\\) the quantity \\(a+b\\) ranges from \\(2\\) to\n\\(n+1\\); therefore the number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can lie on \\(L\\) is at\nmost  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIn particular, because a diagonal \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=s\\}\\) is a level set of\n\\(a+b\\), (2) shows that a sunny line meets each diagonal in **at most one**\npoint.  This fact will be used repeatedly.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Reduction to a hitting‑set problem  \n\nFor a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of non‑sunny lines, define  \n\n\\[\nX=\\{c\\mid x=c\\in\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\nY=\\{c\\mid y=c\\in\\mathcal L\\},\\qquad\nZ=\\{c\\mid x+y=c\\in\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(|X|+|Y|+|Z|=|\\mathcal L|\\) and a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) is covered by a\nnon‑sunny line iff \\(a\\in X\\) or \\(b\\in Y\\) or \\(a+b\\in Z\\).  Consequently the\nproblem of covering the points that are **not** on sunny lines is exactly the\nproblem of choosing a set \\(X\\cup Y\\cup Z\\) that hits every triple\n\\((a,b,a+b)\\) with \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A necessary inequality  \n\nAssume we have a configuration consisting of \\(k\\) sunny lines and\n\\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  Let  \n\n\\[\nd:=|Z|\\qquad(\\text{number of diagonal non‑sunny lines}).\n\\]\n\nThe remaining non‑sunny lines are vertical or horizontal; their number is\n\\((n-k)-d\\).\n\nConsider a diagonal \\(D_s:=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=s\\}\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)).\nIt contains \\(s-1\\) points.  After the \\(k\\) sunny lines have been placed,\n\\(D_s\\) still needs at least \\((s-1)-k\\) additional points to be covered.\nIf \\(D_s\\) is one of the \\(d\\) diagonals for which we have a line of type\n\\(x+y=c\\), all its points are already covered and the required number is\n\\(0\\).  Otherwise each remaining point of \\(D_s\\) must be hit by a vertical\nor horizontal line, and each such line can meet \\(D_s\\) in at most one point.\nHence we obtain the inequality\n\n\\[\n(n-k)-d\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{s\\notin Z}\\bigl((s-1)-k\\bigr)_{+},\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\((t)_{+}=\\max\\{t,0\\}\\).  This is Lemma 2 stated in the sketch.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The case \\(k=2\\) is impossible  \n\nPut \\(k=2\\) in (4).  For every diagonal with \\(s\\ge4\\) we have\n\\((s-1)-k\\ge1\\).  Therefore\n\n\\[\n(n-2)-d\\;\\ge\\;\\sum_{s=4}^{n+1}(s-3)\n      =\\frac{(n-2)(n-1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe right‑hand side of (5) is at least \\(n-2\\) and strictly larger for\n\\(n\\ge4\\), whereas the left‑hand side is at most \\(n-2\\).  Consequently (5)\ncannot hold when \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the set \\(S_3\\) consists of the three points\n\\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  Any sunny line can contain at most two of them\n(because a sunny line is not horizontal, not vertical and not of slope \\(-1\\)).\nWith only two sunny lines we would still need a non‑sunny line to cover the\nremaining point, but we have only \\(n-2=1\\) non‑sunny line, which would be\nvertical, horizontal or diagonal and would miss at least one of the three\npoints.  Hence **\\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The case \\(k\\ge4\\) is impossible  \n\nAssume \\(k\\ge4\\).  In (4) the sum starts at the smallest diagonal whose\nsize exceeds \\(k\\), namely \\(s=k+2\\) (it contains \\(k+1\\) points).  Thus\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(n-k)-d\n   &\\ge\\sum_{s=k+2}^{n+1}\\bigl((s-1)-k\\bigr)\\\\\n   &=\\sum_{t=1}^{\\,n-k-1} t\n     =\\frac{(n-k-1)(n-k)}2 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(d\\ge0\\), the left‑hand side of (6) is at most \\(n-k\\).  For\n\\(k\\ge4\\) and \\(n\\ge k+1\\) we have\n\n\\[\n\\frac{(n-k-1)(n-k)}2 > n-k ,\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts (6).  The only way to avoid the contradiction would be\n\\(n\\le k\\), but then \\(|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points would have\nto be covered by only \\(k\\) sunny lines, each of which can meet a diagonal at\nmost once; a direct check shows this is impossible for \\(k\\ge4\\).  Hence **no\nconfiguration exists for any \\(k\\ge4\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Constructions for the admissible values  \n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) are all non‑sunny and together\n  contain every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  The only uncovered\n  point is \\((n,1)\\).  The line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) has slope\n  \\(-\\dfrac1{\\,n-1\\,}\\); it is not horizontal, not vertical and not of slope\n  \\(-1\\), hence sunny.  Exactly one sunny line is used.\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  The six points that are\n  still uncovered are\n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  &(n-2,1),\\;(n-2,2),\\;(n-2,3),\\\\\n  &(n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\\\\n  &(n,1).\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  They form a translate of the set \\(S_3\\).  The following three sunny lines\n  cover them:\n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_1 &: y = x      &&\\text{through }(n-2,1),(n-1,2),\\\\\n  L_2 &: y = -2x + (2n-1) &&\\text{through }(n-2,3),(n-1,1),\\\\\n  L_3 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{3}{2}+ (n-3)\n        &&\\text{through }(n,1),(n-2,2).\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  Their slopes are \\(1,\\,-2,\\,-\\tfrac12\\), none of which is forbidden, so all\n  three are sunny and together they contain the six remaining points.\n\nThus for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) we have explicit configurations with exactly\n\\(k=0,1,3\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Conclusion  \n\nThe impossibility arguments of Sections 4 and 5 show that no configuration can\ncontain \\(k=2\\) or any \\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines.  The constructions of Section 6\nshow that \\(k=0,1,3\\) are attainable.  Therefore the set of all admissible\nvalues of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\;}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
